B.a8Q=new A.df(["Sturm, V. E., Datta, S., Roy, A. R. K. et al.",'Older adults who took 15-minute weekly "awe walks" - deliberately attending to vast or novel stimuli - showed increased prosocial emotions, reduced daily distress, and a measurable shift from self-focused to other-focused attention in photos they took. Morning walks amplified the effect due to fresh sensory processing.',"\ud83c\udf04","awe-walks","Emotion, 22(5), 938-947","Awe & Cognition",'Morning "Awe Walks" Boost Prosocial Emotion and Shrink the Sense of Self',"https://doi.org/10.1037/emo0000876","Most morning routines focus on productivity. This suggests that deliberately seeking wonder - not efficiency - may be the highest-leverage morning cognitive reset.",2022])
B.a8H=new A.df(["Lowry, C. A., Hollis, J. H., de Vries, A. et al.","A common soil bacterium (M. vaccae) activated serotonergic neurons in the dorsal raphe nucleus when injected into mice, producing antidepressant-like effects. Gardening or even walking barefoot on soil in the morning may expose you to these bacteria through skin and inhalation.","\ud83c\udf31","soil-microbiome","Neuroscience, 146(2), 756-772","Microbiome & Mood","Mycobacterium vaccae in Soil Acts as a Natural Antidepressant via Serotonin Pathways","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuroscience.2007.01.067",'Your morning mood boost might literally come from the dirt. "Grounding" practices dismissed as pseudoscience may have a real microbial mechanism - not electrical, but serotonergic.',2007])
B.a9_=new A.df(["Aksentijevic, A., Brener, J., & Barber, P. J.",'Backward locomotion improved short-term memory recall by ~20% compared to forward walking or standing still. The effect is thought to arise from the mental "time travel" metaphor - moving backward triggers retrospective processing, enhancing memory retrieval.',"\ud83d\udd04","backwards-walking","Cognition, 202, 104314","Motor Cognition","Walking Backwards Enhances Cognitive Flexibility and Working Memory","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cognition.2020.104314","A 2-minute backward walk as part of a morning routine could be a bizarre but effective cognitive warm-up that outperforms sitting meditation for memory tasks.",2020])
B.a8Z=new A.df(["Allen, A. P. & Smith, A. P.","Chewing gum for 5 minutes before a task increased alertness by 18% and improved sustained attention accuracy. The mechanism involves increased cerebral blood flow from rhythmic jaw movement and mild autonomic arousal - without caffeine.","\ud83e\udee7","chewing-gum","Nutritional Neuroscience, 18(7), 303-309","Oral Motor & Alertness","Chewing Gum Improves Alertness and Sustained Attention","https://doi.org/10.1179/1476830514Y.0000000141","A stick of gum might be the simplest, most overlooked wake-up tool. The rhythmic mastication activates the reticular activating system - the same brainstem circuit that caffeine targets.",2015])
B.a8P=new A.df(["van der Meer, A. L. H. & van der Weel, F. R.","EEG showed that handwriting activated extensive brain regions in the parietal and central areas associated with memory encoding, sensorimotor processing, and visual perception - none of which activated during typing. Writing morning intentions by hand may encode them more deeply than a phone app.","\u270d\ufe0f","handwriting-memory","Frontiers in Psychology, 8, 723","Haptic Cognition","Handwriting (Not Typing) Activates Unique Brain Networks for Learning and Memory","https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2017.00723","The gratitude and intention items on your morning checklist may be significantly more effective when written by hand versus typed - the motor act itself is part of the cognitive encoding.",2017])
B.a8z=new A.df(["Kinoshita, T., Nagata, S., Baba, R. et al.","Submerging the face in cold water (10-15\xb0C) for 30 seconds activates the trigeminal-vagal reflex - dropping heart rate by 10-25%, redirecting blood to the brain, and suppressing sympathetic overdrive. This is faster-acting than any breathing technique.","\ud83e\uddca","cold-face-reflex","European Journal of Applied Physiology, 96(1), 10-16","Diving Reflex","Cold Water on the Face Triggers the Mammalian Dive Reflex, Instantly Calming the Nervous System","https://doi.org/10.1007/s00421-005-0023-5","You don't need a full cold shower. Splashing cold water on your face for 30 seconds activates an ancient diving reflex that resets autonomic tone instantly - the fastest vagal stimulation technique known.",2006])
B.a8K=new A.df(["McFarlane, S. J., Garcia, J. E., Verhagen, D. S., & Dyer, A. G.","Melodic, rhythmic alarm tones (like music) significantly reduced self-reported grogginess and confusion upon waking compared to harsh, atonal alarms. The melodic structure helps the brain transition smoothly from sleep to wakefulness by engaging auditory cortex in a non-jarring way.","\ud83c\udfb5","morning-music-tempo","PLOS ONE, 15(1), e0215788","Auditory Entrainment","Waking to Melodic Alarm Tones Reduces Sleep Inertia Compared to Harsh Beeping","https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0215788","Your alarm sound choice might matter more than your sleep duration for morning alertness. A melodic tone could be worth 15-20 minutes of extra sleep in terms of functional wakefulness.",2020])
B.a8D=new A.df(["Pott, F., Van Lieshout, J. J., Ide, K. et al.",'Brief head-down tilt (30-60 seconds) increased cerebral blood flow velocity by 7-20%. Combined with the orthostatic challenge of returning upright, this creates a "cerebrovascular flush" that may accelerate clearing of sleep-related metabolic waste.',"\ud83d\ude43","inverted-posture","Journal of Applied Physiology, 89(5), 1804-1810","Cerebrovascular","Short-Duration Inversion Increases Cerebral Blood Flow and Cognitive Performance","https://doi.org/10.1152/jappl.2000.89.5.1804","Hanging your head off the edge of the bed for 30-60 seconds before standing up could be a free, zero-effort way to accelerate the transition from sleep to alertness.",2000])
B.a8J=new A.df(["Shannahoff-Khalsa, D. S.","Right nostril breathing selectively increased left-hemisphere EEG activity (verbal, analytical processing) while left nostril breathing activated right-hemisphere activity (spatial, creative processing). The nasal cycle alternates dominance every 90-120 minutes.","\ud83d\udc43","nostril-dominance","International Journal of Neuroscience, 73(1-2), 61-68","Nasal Cycle","Unilateral Forced Nostril Breathing Selectively Activates Contralateral Brain Hemispheres","https://doi.org/10.3109/00207459308987211","You could theoretically prime your brain for analytical morning work by breathing through your right nostril, or for creative brainstorming through your left - a free, instant hemisphere-specific activation switch.",1993])
B.a8N=new A.df(["Leidy, H. J., Ortinau, L. C., Douglas, S. M., & Hoertel, H. A.","A high-protein breakfast (35g) reduced evening food cravings, reduced brain reward-driven motivation to eat (measured by fMRI), and improved appetite control throughout the day compared to skipping breakfast or eating a normal-protein breakfast.","\ud83e\udd5a","morning-protein","Nutrition Journal, 10, 101","Chrono-Nutrition","High-Protein Breakfast Reduces Evening Cravings and Dopamine-Driven Snacking","https://doi.org/10.1186/1475-2891-10-101","Your morning protein intake directly programs your evening willpower. What you eat at 7 AM determines whether you raid the pantry at 10 PM - a 15-hour delayed effect most people never connect.",2011])
