B.a8V=new A.df(["Dewar, M., Alber, J., Butler, C., Cowan, N., & Della Sala, S.","Participants who sat quietly for 10 minutes after learning new information retained 10-30% more material a week later compared to those who immediately started a new task. The resting brain replays and consolidates recent experiences - interrupting this with phone scrolling or task-switching destroys the consolidation window.","\u23f8\ufe0f","micro-breaks-cognition","Psychological Science, 23(9), 955-960","Wakeful Rest","Brief Wakeful Resting After Learning Boosts Memory Retention by 10-30%","https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797612441220","The most productive thing you can do after learning something important is literally nothing. Staring out the window for 10 minutes beats any note-taking app for memory.",2012])
B.a8X=new A.df(["Lee, K. E., Williams, K. J. H., Sargent, L. D., Williams, N. S. G., & Johnson, K. A.",'Just 40 seconds of viewing a flowering green roof from a window restored sustained attention performance to near-baseline levels, while viewing a concrete roof did not. The effect is driven by "soft fascination" - nature engages attention effortlessly, allowing directed attention circuits to recover.',"\ud83c\udf33","nature-attention-restoration","Journal of Environmental Psychology, 42, 182-189","Attention Restoration","A 40-Second Micro-Break Viewing a Green Roof Restores Sustained Attention","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvp.2015.04.003","You don't need a 20-minute walk in the park. A 40-second glance at anything green and natural - even a photo - measurably restores your focus. The threshold is absurdly low.",2015])
B.a8M=new A.df([u.m,"Breaking up sitting with 3-minute walking bouts every 30 minutes reduced postprandial glucose and insulin responses by 30-40% more effectively than a single 30-minute morning walk followed by prolonged sitting. The metabolic benefit comes from frequency of muscle activation, not total exercise volume.","\ud83d\udeb6","incidental-movement","Diabetes Care, 39(6), 964-972","Movement Snacking","Frequent Short Walking Bouts Outperform Continuous Exercise for Blood Sugar Control","https://doi.org/10.2337/dc15-2336",'Your dedicated morning workout may be undone by 8 hours of sitting. Tiny "movement snacks" throughout the day are metabolically superior to a single concentrated exercise session.',2016])
B.a8T=new A.df(["Moser, J. S., Dougherty, A., Mattson, W. I. et al.",'Using third-person self-talk ("Rob is feeling stressed about this deadline") during emotional situations reduced emotional brain reactivity within 1 second, with no more cognitive effort than first-person self-talk. ERP data showed the shift happened automatically, not through effortful regulation.',"\ud83e\ude9e","self-distancing","Scientific Reports, 7, 4519","Meta-Cognition","Referring to Yourself in Third Person Reduces Emotional Reactivity in Real-Time","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-04047-3","Talking about yourself as if you're narrating someone else's life isn't crazy - it's instantaneous emotional regulation. The psychological distance created by \"he/she\" vs \"I\" fundamentally changes how the brain processes threats.",2017])
B.a8U=new A.df([u._,"Elite performers across domains (music, chess, athletics) structured deliberate practice into ~90-minute sessions with breaks - matching the basic rest-activity cycle (BRAC). Exceeding 90 minutes of focused effort without a break leads to disproportionate performance drops due to ultradian rhythm phase shifts.","\ud83d\udd01","ultradian-rhythm","Psychological Review, 100(3), 363-406","Ultradian Cycles","The Brain Operates in 90-Minute Focus Cycles - Working Against Them Degrades Performance",u.bZ,'The "hustle culture" 4-hour deep work block fights your neurobiology. Your brain has a built-in 90-minute timer. The most elite performers on Earth work with it, not against it.',1993])
B.a8G=new A.df(["Nair, S., Sagar, M., Sollers, J., Consedine, N., & Broadbent, E.","Participants assigned to sit upright during a stressful task reported higher self-esteem, more arousal, better mood, and lower fear compared to slumped participants. Upright posture also produced more positive and fewer negative words in speech. The body-to-brain signal is direct and immediate.","\ud83c\udfdb\ufe0f","posture-hormones","Health Psychology, 34(6), 632-641","Embodied Cognition","Upright Posture During Stress Maintains Self-Esteem and Reduces Negative Mood","https://doi.org/10.1037/hea0000146","Your posture isn't just about back health - it's a real-time input to your emotional processing. Sitting upright during a difficult email or conversation literally changes your brain's threat assessment.",2015])
B.a8W=new A.df(["Hayashi, M., Masuda, A., & Hori, T.","Drinking coffee immediately before a 15-20 minute nap resulted in significantly better post-nap alertness and performance than either caffeine alone or napping alone. Caffeine takes ~20 minutes to reach peak blood concentration - the same duration as an optimal power nap - so you wake up to a double boost.","\u2615","strategic-caffeine","Clinical Neurophysiology, 114(12), 2268-2278","Chrono-Pharmacology","Caffeine Naps: Coffee Immediately Before a 20-Min Nap Outperforms Either Alone","https://doi.org/10.1016/S1388-2457(03)00255-4",'It sounds contradictory: drink a stimulant then immediately sleep. But the pharmacokinetics align perfectly - the caffeine "arrives" right as you wake, stacking on top of sleep inertia reduction. The timing exploit is elegant.',2003])
B.a8F=new A.df(["Rohrer, D., Dedrick, R. F., & Stencil, K.","Students who interleaved practice across different problem types scored 72% on a delayed test versus 38% for students who practiced each type in blocks. Interleaving feels harder and slower during practice, creating an illusion of poor learning - but the long-term retention is nearly doubled.","\ud83d\udd00","interleaving-learning","Journal of Educational Psychology, 107(3), 900-908","Desirable Difficulty","Mixing Up Practice Topics (Interleaving) Dramatically Outperforms Blocked Practice","https://doi.org/10.1037/edu0000029","The study method that feels most productive (focused blocks) produces the worst long-term results. The method that feels confusing and slow (mixing topics) nearly doubles retention. Your feelings about learning are anti-correlated with actual learning.",2015])
B.a8A=new A.df(["Rudd, M., Vohs, K. D., & Aaker, J.","Brief awe experiences (watching a nature video, recalling a past awe moment) made participants feel they had more time available, increased willingness to volunteer, boosted life satisfaction, and shifted preference from material goods to experiences. Awe literally makes time feel less scarce.","\u2728","awe-time-perception","Psychological Science, 23(10), 1130-1136","Time Perception","Experiencing Awe Expands Subjective Time Perception and Reduces Impatience","https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797612438731","When you feel time-starved and rushed, the counterintuitive fix is to stop and experience something vast - a sunset, a massive tree, a stunning piece of architecture. The perception of expanded time makes you more patient, generous, and satisfied.",2012])
B.a8L=new A.df(["Kjaer, T. W., Bertelsen, C., Piccini, P. et al.","Yoga Nidra meditation (lying still with guided body scanning, ~20 minutes) increased endogenous dopamine release in the ventral striatum by 65% as measured by PET scan. This wasn't from movement, reward, or stimulation - the brain generated its own dopamine through deep, directed relaxation.","\ud83e\uddd8","non-sleep-deep-rest","Cognitive Brain Research, 13(2), 255-259","NSDR Protocol","Yoga Nidra (Non-Sleep Deep Rest) Replenishes Dopamine by 65% in the Striatum","https://doi.org/10.1016/S0926-6410(01)00106-9","When your motivation tanks at 2 PM, the fix isn't more caffeine or willpower - it's lying down and doing a 20-minute body scan. You can replenish your dopamine reserves without any external input.",2002])
