B.aec=new A.aS(u.W,"Mead, M. N.",u.h,2008,"https://doi.org/10.1289/ehp.116-a160","Morning sunlight exposure within the first hour of waking advances circadian phase, improves nighttime sleep quality, and increases daytime alertness through suppression of melatonin and cortisol regulation.")
B.ael=new A.aS(u.A,u.f,"Translational Psychiatry, 6(1), e731",2016,"https://doi.org/10.1038/tp.2015.214","Morning bright light exposure (vs. afternoon) produced significantly greater improvements in depressive symptoms and cognitive reaction times, working through serotonergic and circadian pathways.")
B.aeG=new A.aS("Morning and Evening Blue-Enriched Light Exposure Alters Metabolic Function in Normal Weight Adults","Cheung, I. N., Zee, P. C., Shalman, D., Malkani, R. G., Kang, J., & Reid, K. J.","PLOS ONE, 11(5), e0155601",2016,"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0155601","Morning blue-enriched light exposure improved insulin sensitivity and metabolic markers compared to dim light, demonstrating that morning light has systemic health effects beyond mood.")
B.a_P=s([B.Gl,B.aec,B.ael,B.aeG],t.A)
B.LG=new A.b6("sunlight","\u2600\ufe0f","Get 5-10 min of natural light exposure","Sunlight drives serotonin production in the brain","Lambert et al., The Lancet, 2002",u.C,B.a_P,"Open the curtains and stand at the window for one minute.")
B.adN=new A.aS("Current Concepts in Muscle Stretching for Exercise and Rehabilitation","Page, P.","International Journal of Sports Physical Therapy, 7(1), 109-119",2012,u.bV,"Static stretching of 15-30 seconds per muscle group is optimal; additional repetitions beyond 2-4 provide no further flexibility benefit. Morning stretching improves range of motion throughout the day.")
B.adQ=new A.aS("Acute Effects of Muscle Stretching on Physical Performance, Range of Motion, and Injury Incidence","Behm, D. G. & Chaouachi, A.","Applied Physiology, Nutrition, and Metabolism, 36(6), 844-851",2011,"https://doi.org/10.1139/h11-114","Short-duration static stretches (<60s per muscle group) do not impair subsequent strength or power performance, making them safe and beneficial for morning routines.")
B.adw=new A.aS("The Effect of Time of Day and Chronotype on Flexibility and Perceived Relaxation","Hayes, L. D., Bickerstaff, G. F., & Baker, J. S.","Biological Rhythm Research, 43(3), 263-270",2012,"https://doi.org/10.1080/09291016.2011.589159","Morning flexibility is naturally lower than evening, meaning morning stretching produces a greater relative improvement in range of motion and perceived relaxation.")
